///|
/// Loop #85: making a private key file private.
///
/// A private key file that other users can read is not a private key
/// file.  OpenSSH says so out loud -- its `ssh-keygen -y` refuses to
/// read a key whose permissions are too open, with the message
/// "Permissions for '...' are too open.  It is required that your private
/// key files are NOT accessible by others." -- and it is right.
///
/// This package exists because writing the file is not enough:
/// **a file created on Windows inherits the directory's ACL**, and in a
/// normal user directory that ACL lets `Authenticated Users` read it.
/// So a key written by `cmd/ssh_keygen` would be a key that OpenSSH
/// refuses to use and that any other process on the machine could read.
///
/// The fix here is the Windows one, because Windows is where this
/// repository runs: the file's inherited ACL is replaced by one that grants
/// the owner alone, with `ConvertStringSecurityDescriptorToSecurityDescriptorW`
/// and `SetFileSecurityW` -- `keyperm.c` says why it took six attempts to
/// get there.
///
/// On POSIX the exclusive create mode is what makes a file private, and
/// there is no ACL to set; the js and wasm targets have no way to protect a
/// file at all, and `protect` says so there instead of pretending.
///
/// **A failure to tighten is a failure, not a warning.**  The caller is
/// writing a secret; a key file that was written and could not be
/// protected is a key file that must not be reported as done.  So
/// `protect` reports what happened and the driver says so.
import {
}

pkgtype(kind: "library")

options(
  native_stub: [ "keyperm.c" ],
  targets: {
    "keyperm_native.mbt": [ "native" ],
    "keyperm_stub.mbt": [ "js", "wasm", "wasm-gc" ],
  },
)
